{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TUcVEC3694Xci7SxMadxAu","type":"tool_result","content":"=== paper1 ack\n% Acknowledgements, with the disclosure of AI use arXiv asks authors to make\n% (written 2026-10-15 at the owner's request). Software versions from the\n% repository: lean/lean-toolchain, the Python environment, Appendix D.\n\\section*{Acknowledgements}\n\\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Acknowledgements}\n\n\\paragraph{Use of AI tools.} Much of this work was carried out with Claude Code\n(Anthropic), an AI coding assistant running Claude models. Under the authors'\ndirection it wrote most of the C and Python software, the Lean proofs and drafts of the\ntext, ran the computations, and also searched and summarised the literature. The\nauthors chose the questions, directed the work, read the results and take full\nresponsibility for the content of this paper. We did not take the AI's word for any result: every theorem the paper states\nis checked by the Lean kernel, except the two that the limits of\nSection~\\ref{sec:closing} name as not formalised; every value in\nthe dataset carries a witness that an independent checker verifies; and the\nrefutations behind the central claims (the wrong optimum of\nTheorem~\\ref{thm:wrong}, and the minor bounds for the Mycielski graphs and four\nMOSP instances) are re-checked by methods independent of our search: a SAT solver with a DRAT\nproof that drat-trim verifies, and a separate exact search with its own\nchecker, written from scratch (\\repofile{reports/minor_bound_verification.md},\n\\repofile{reports/scratch/minor_certificates/} and\n\\repofile{tests/test_false_refutation_instance.py}).\nThe other refutations rest on our corrected search, whose rules are proved\nsound in Lean (Sections \\ref{sec:search} and \\ref{sec:results}).\n\n\\paragraph{Software.} The formal proofs use Lean~4 \\citep{MouraUllrich2021},\nversion 4.34, with the Mathlib library \\citep{Mathlib2020}. The SAT checks use\nKissat~4.0.4 (\\url{https://github.com/arminbiere/kissat}) and\nCaDiCaL~1.9.5 \\citep{CaDiCaL2024} through PySAT \\citep{PySAT2018}, and the\nproofs of unsatisfiability are checked by drat-trim \\citep{WetzlerHeuleHunt2014}.\nIsomorphism classes are computed with nauty \\citep{McKayPiperno2014}, through\npynauty, and the graphs are handled with NetworkX. The bibliometric counts use\nOpenAlex \\citep{Priem2022}. SageMath provides the openly available\nimplementation of the branch and bound of \\citet{CoudertMazauricNisse2016}.\n=== paper1 data stmt\npaper1/latex/sec7_data.tex\n% The data, code and proofs statement (loop0009 item 01). It replaces the\n% title-page footnote and every file path and `python -m` command that the\n% body used to carry. Those commands, kept here for later items and recorded\n% in the paper1/*.md documents that are the sources of record:\n%   Section 3 figures and numbers  python -m paper1.section2_figures [--numbers]\n%                                  (paper1/relevance.py, paper1/trends.py; offline,\n%                                  from the OpenAlex caches of 29 September 2026)\n%   Tables (generated)             python -m paper1.latex.make_tables\n%   Band values (brute force)      python -m paper1.complex_check\n%   Figure 4.1                     python -m paper1.counterexample_figure\n%   Validation counts, Section 4.4 paper1/solver_fix.md items 01-05;\n%                                  paper1/revised_algorithm.md section 4.4.4\n%   Prior reports                  python3 -m paper1.prior_art_sweep\n%                                  (paper1/prior_art_counterexample.md)\n%   Split re-certification         python -m paper1.solver_fix_split --summary\n%                                  (Lean: Search/Split.lean)\n%   Dataset                        python -m paper1.dataset (builder);\n%                                  python paper1/dataset_check.py --sources (checker)\n%   Axiom audit                    lake env lean paper1/axiom_check.lean\n\\section*{Data, code and proofs}\n\\label{sec:data}\n\\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Data, code and proofs}\n\nOur code, proofs and data are openly available at\n\\url{https://github.com/Alex-Linhares/MOSP_trough_SAT}; the code and the proofs\nare open source under the MIT licence. Everything this paper reports can be\nre-run from there, and most of it can be checked\nwithout re-running. The Lean proofs check in one build. Witnesses, minor\ncertificates and the 34-customer instance have independent checkers, and\nrefutations up to about 75 customers carry certificates\n(Section~\\ref{sec:certs}). The largest refutations demand enormous certificates and, hence, can only be\nre-run, at up to 2,231 core-hours each\n(Section~\\ref{sec:practice}). The repository holds:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item the Lean~4 development, with every theorem the paper names\n  (Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}) and an axiom check that covers all of them;\n\\item the source of both our solvers, the open-stacks customer search and the\n  graph pathwidth solver, in C and in Python, with our corrected rules as the\n  default and the rules of the previous algorithm \\citep{ChuStuckey2009} as an\n  option;\n\\item the graph pathwidth solver as a standalone package\n  (\\repofile{pathwidth_solver/}), with the scripts that produced\n  Table~\\ref{tab:graphs} and the published values it is compared with;\n\\item the dataset of Section~\\ref{sec:dataset}, the certificates of\n  Section~\\ref{sec:certs} and the independent checkers of both; the benchmark\n  graphs keep the terms of their original distributions;\n\\item the 34-customer instance of Figure~\\ref{fig:wrong}, with the optimum our\n  implementation of the previous algorithm reports with the better move off and\n  its order, an optimal order, a checker that uses only the Python standard\n  library, and the DRAT refutation of 5 stacks with the script that checks it\n  (\\repofile{paper1/data/false_refutation/});\n\\item the bibliometric data of Section~\\ref{sec:names}: the cached OpenAlex\n  responses, the relevance labels and the mechanical rule;\n\\item a script for every table, figure and number in the paper, each of which\n  regenerates it from the recorded data.\n\\end{itemize}\n=== main.tex head\n10:% In \\title below `$2^n$` and `\\ldots` are its only LaTeX; the pdftitle carries\n12:% about the wording go to ../ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD, never into the title.\n16:\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n33:\\usepackage[title,titletoc]{appendix}\n34:\\usepackage[authoryear,round]{natbib}\n36:\\hypersetup{pdftitle={Exponential time hypothesis is false... but the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2\\string^n assignments at every practical size.}}\n49:\\newcommand{\\leannames}[1]{\\def\\lean@sep{}\\@for\\lean@n:=#1\\do{\\lean@sep\\expandafter\\path\\expandafter{\\lean@n}\\def\\lean@sep{, }}}\n52:\\renewcommand{\\theleanentry}{A.\\arabic{leanentry}}\n53:\\newcommand{\\leanentry}[2]{\\leavevmode\\refstepcounter{leanentry}\\label{lean:#1}\\theleanentry & #2 &}\n54:\\newcommand{\\leangroup}[1]{\\multicolumn{3}{@{}p{\\dimexpr\\linewidth-2\\tabcolsep\\relax}@{}}{\\emph{#1}}\\\\}\n55:\\newcommand{\\leanref}[1]{(Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}, \\ref{lean:#1})}\n69:\\newcommand{\\rhon}{\\rho(n)}\n70:\\newcommand{\\runtimeScale}{\\texttt{runtimeScale}}\n71:\\newcommand{\\SubexpCount}{\\texttt{SubexpCount}}\n72:% Paths in the repository and in the clone; a placeholder box for text an item\n74:\\newcommand{\\repofile}[1]{\\path{#1}}\n75:\\newcommand{\\clonefile}[1]{\\path{#1}}\n76:\\newcommand{\\placeholder}[1]{\\par\\noindent\\fbox{\\parbox{\\dimexpr\\linewidth-2\\fboxsep-2\\fboxrule}{\\textbf{Placeholder.} #1}}\\par}\n79:\\title{Exponential time hypothesis is false\\ldots{} but the verified algorithm is slower than trying all $2^n$ assignments at every practical size.}\n83:\\author{Alex Linhares\\\\{\\small Argo Lab AGI; on leave from the Getulio Vargas Foundation}\\\\{\\small \\href{mailto:alex@argolab.org}{alex@argola