{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017j8T6YDnEYyMn3pPHj6QW6","type":"tool_result","content":"=== paper1 bib Lean/Mathlib\n538:@inproceedings{MouraUllrich2021,\n539-  author    = {de Moura, Leonardo and Ullrich, Sebastian},\n540-  title     = {The {Lean} 4 Theorem Prover and Programming Language},\n541-  booktitle = {Automated Deduction -- CADE 28},\n542-  series    = {Lecture Notes in Computer Science},\n543-  volume    = {12699},\n544-  pages     = {625--635},\n545-  year      = {2021},\n546-  doi       = {10.1007/978-3-030-79876-5_37}\n547-}\n548-\n549:@inproceedings{Mathlib2020,\n550-  author    = {{The mathlib Community}},\n551-  title     = {The {Lean} Mathematical Library},\n552-  booktitle = {Proceedings of the 9th ACM SIGPLAN International Conference on Certified Programs and Proofs (CPP 2020)},\n553-  pages     = {367--381},\n554-  year      = {2020},\n555-  doi       = {10.1145/3372885.3373824}\n556-}\n557-\n558-@inproceedings{CaDiCaL2024,\n559-  author    = {Biere, Armin and Faller, Tobias and Fazekas, Katalin and Fleury, Mathias and Froleyks, Nils and Pollitt, Florian},\n=== budget\n7:BUDGET = 4000.0  # owner, 2026-11-05: $2,500 for loop0015\n17:    (HERE / \"budget.txt\").write_text(f\"{time.strftime('%F %T')} summed cost ${total:.2f} of ${BUDGET:.0f}\\n\")\n18:    if total > BUDGET:\n2026-11-05 08:36:31 summed cost $133.20 of $4000\n{\n  \"iteration_cap_hours\": 4.0,\n  \"fix_cap_hours\": 0.75,\n  \"max_fix_attempts\": 3,\n  \"max_iterations\": 24,\n  \"model\": null,\n  \"pause\": false,\n  \"stop\": false,\n  \"sleep_between_s\": 0,\n  \"run_tests\": true,\n  \"retry_on_error\": 1,\n  \"retry_wait_s\": 300\n}\n=== abstract\n\n\\begin{abstract}\n\\noindent\n% Item 09 (2026-11-05). Under 200 words; the only numbers are 50 and\n% the 2,391 digits of e^5504, both printed by paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py\n% (Section 2); the only formula is 2^n (owner's rule of 2026-11-05 05:30).\nIn October 2026 OpenAI published a theorem, checked by the Lean proof\nassistant, that refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis: the 2001\nconjecture that deciding whether a formula with three variables per\nclause can be satisfied needs time exponential in the number of\nvariables. We read the verified statement exactly, rebuilt its proof in\nisolation, re-checked it with a second, independent checker, and found\nno flaw. It does refute the hypothesis. What we add is exactness about\n\\emph{what} is verified. The theorem is a guarantee, not a running time:\nit says that a machine and some constants exist, and never computes\nthem. Its exponent, the power of two in the bound, is the one the\nmanuscript itself calls weaker; the manuscript's sharper exponents are\nnot in the Lean library. With every constant set to one, that exponent\nexceeds the number of variables from 50 variables up to a number with\n2,391 digits. So at every practical size the verified guarantee is\nweaker than the guarantee of trying all $2^n$ assignments. We say what\nfollows for the hypotheses, for the lower bounds proved from them, and\nfor anyone who runs a program on such formulas: nothing changes on any\nmachine.\n\\end{abstract}\n=== intro claims\n\\subsection{What is claimed, and what is not}\n\\label{sec:intro-claims}\n\nClaimed, each with the section that establishes it:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item The verified statement is a running-time \\emph{guarantee} with\n  constants that exist and are never computed, for each fixed clause\n  width, with no lower limit on the number of variables\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\\item With every constant set to one, the verified exponent exceeds the\n  number of variables at every size from 50 up to a number with 2,391\n  digits, so the verified bound is above $2^n$ there. The true constants\n  are unknown and at least 257 in the proof on record, so with them the\n  bound is higher still (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\\item The manuscript's two sharper exponents, and the circuit bound that\n  rests on one of them, are not in the Lean library\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n\\item The proof's source shows none of the known ways of weakening a\n  verified proof, and its statement is the challenge's\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). It builds from source at the pinned\n  version with the three standard axioms, and a second checker agrees\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}).\n\\item The verified statement refutes ETH, SETH, their gap, guessing and\n  counting forms, and the randomised forms of ETH, SETH and the gap\n  form, by the parts of the statement named in each case. A lower bound\n  derived from any of them loses its premise and nothing more\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:consequences}).\n\\item On random formulas, the solvers a practitioner has are many\n  orders of magnitude below $2^n$ at every size measured, and the\n  verified guarantee is above $2^n$ from 50 variables on. The verified\n  result changes nothing that runs (Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}).\n\\end{enumerate}\nNot claimed:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item No running time of the verified machine, in either direction. No\n  implementation of it exists; the title compares two guarantees.\n\\item No flaw in the statement, the proof, the Lean kernel, Mathlib, or\n  the collection's checking tool. We found none and assert none.\n\\item No value of any constant beyond what the proof writes down, and\n  nothing about what a different proof of the same statement could\n  achieve.\n\\item No judgement on the manuscript's paper-only proofs, which were\n  located and quoted, not read for correctness.\n\\item No refutation of any lower bound's conclusion, for any problem,\n  and nothing about any specific problem's complexity beyond what the\n  manuscript's own remarks state.\n\\item Nothing about any problem outside satisfiability. This paper is\n  about the limitation of the ETH refutation and nothing else.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection{How the paper is organised}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6bd812a1-f67e-4602-8dec-e9f4b911534a","uuid":"f81f1d9b-ff1b-484c-829b-8015da24dccd","timestamp":"2026-11-05T08:39:01.889Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"=== paper1 bib Lean/Mathlib\n538:@inproceedings{MouraUllrich2021,\n539-  author    = {de Moura, Leonardo and Ullrich, Sebastian},\n540-  title     = {The {Lean} 4 Theorem Prover and Programming Language},\n541-  booktitle = {Automated Deduction -- CADE 28},\n542-  series    = {Lecture Notes in Computer Science},\n543-  volume    = {12699},\n544-  pages     = {625--635},\n545-  year      = {2021},\n546-  doi       = {10.1007/978-3-030-79876-5_37}\n547-}\n548-\n549:@inproceedings{Mathlib2020,\n550-  author    = {{The mathlib Community}},\n551-  title     = {The {Lean} Mathematical Library},\n552-  booktitle = {Proceedings of the 9th ACM SIGPLAN International Conference on Certified Programs and Proofs (CPP 2020)},\n553-  pages     = {367--381},\n554-  year      = {2020},\n555-  doi       = {10.1145/3372885.3373824}\n556-}\n557-\n558-@inproceedings{CaDiCaL2024,\n559-  author    = {Biere, Armin and Faller, Tobias and Fazekas, Katalin and Fleury, Mathias and Froleyks, Nils and Pollitt, Florian},\n=== budget\n7:BUDGET = 4000.0  # owner, 2026-11-05: $2,500 for loop0015\n17:    (HERE / \"budget.txt\").write_text(f\"{time.strftime('%F %T')} summed cost ${total:.2f} of ${BUDGET:.0f}\\n\")\n18:    if total > BUDGET:\n2026-11-05 08:36:31 summed cost $133.20 of $4000\n{\n  \"iteration_cap_hours\": 4.0,\n  \"fix_cap_hours\": 0.75,\n  \"max_fix_attempts\": 3,\n  \"max_iterations\": 24,\n  \"model\": null,\n  \"pause\": false,\n  \"stop\": false,\n  \"sleep_between_s\": 0,\n  \"run_tests\": true,\n  \"retry_on_error\": 1,\n  \"retry_wait_s\": 300\n}\n=== abstract\n\n\\begin{abstract}\n\\noindent\n% Item 09 (2026-11-05). Under 200 words; the only numbers are 50 and\n% the 2,391 digits of e^5504, both printed by paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py\n% (Section 2); the only formula is 2^n (owner's rule of 2026-11-05 05:30).\nIn October 2026 OpenAI published a theorem, checked by the Lean proof\nassistant, that refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis: the 2001\nconjecture that deciding whether a formula with three variables per\nclause can be satisfied needs time exponential in the number of\nvariables. We read the verified statement exactly, rebuilt its proof in\nisolation, re-checked it with a second, independent checker, and found\nno flaw. It does refute the hypothesis. What we add is exactness about\n\\emph{what} is verified. The theorem is a guarantee, not a running time:\nit says that a machine and some constants exist, and never computes\nthem. Its exponent, the power of two in the bound, is the one the\nmanuscript itself calls weaker; the manuscript's sharper exponents are\nnot in the Lean library. With every constant set to one, that exponent\nexceeds the number of variables from 50 variables up to a number with\n2,391 digits. So at every practical size the verified guarantee is\nweaker than the guarantee of trying all $2^n$ assignments. We say what\nfollows for the hypotheses, for the lower bounds proved from them, and\nfor anyone who runs a program on such formulas: nothing changes on any\nmachine.\n\\end{abstract}\n=== intro claims\n\\subsection{What is claimed, and what is not}\n\\label{sec:intro-claims}\n\nClaimed, each with the section that establishes it:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item The verified statement is a running-time \\emph{guarantee} with\n  constants that exist and are never computed, for each fixed clause\n  width, with no lower limit on the number of variables\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\\item With every constant set to one, the verified exponent exceeds the\n  number of variables at every size from 50 up to a number with 2,391\n  digits, so the verified bound is above $2^n$ there. The true constants\n  are unknown and at least 257 in the proof on record, so with them the\n  bound is higher still (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).\n\\item The manuscript's two sharper exponents, and the circuit bound that\n  rests on one of them, are not in the Lean library\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).\n\\item The proof's source shows none of the known ways of weakening a\n  verified proof, and its statement is the challenge's\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). It builds from source at the pinned\n  version with the three standard axioms, and a second checker agrees\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}).\n\\item The verified statement refutes ETH, SETH, their gap, guessing and\n  counting forms, and the randomised forms of ETH, SETH and the gap\n  form, by the parts of the statement named in each case. A lower bound\n  derived from any of them loses its premise and nothing more\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:consequences}).\n\\item On random formulas, the solvers a practitioner has are many\n  orders of magnitude below $2^n$ at every size measured, and the\n  verified guarantee is above $2^n$ from 50 variables on. The verified\n  result changes nothing that runs (Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}).\n\\end{enumerate}\nNot claimed:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item No running time of the verified machine, in either direction. No\n  implementation of it exists; the title compares two guarantees.\n\\item No flaw in the statement, the proof, the Lean kernel, Mathlib, or\n  the collection's checking tool. We found none and assert none.\n\\item No value of any constant beyond what the proof writes down, and\n  nothing about what a different proof of the same statement could\n  achieve.\n\\item No judgement on the manuscript's paper-only proofs, which were\n  located and quoted, not read for correctness.\n\\item No refutation of any lower bound's conclusion, for any problem,\n  and nothing about any specific problem's complexity beyond what the\n  manuscript's own remarks state.\n\\item Nothing about any problem outside satisfiability. This paper is\n  about the limitation of the ETH refutation and nothing else.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection{How the paper is organised}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_017j8T6YDnEYyMn3pPHj6QW6","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
