{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgjUneMCydmEqP3P5MYWK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017HpqQkG4sp8e2aMhCi2Ddv","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat > paper3/latex/sec11_data.tex <<'EOF'\n% The data, code and proofs statement, in paper 1's form (item 11, 2026-11-05).\n% Every path is in the repository; every number here is a section's and is\n% regenerated by the script that section names (paper3/README.md, \"How to\n% regenerate every number\").\n\\section*{Data, code and proofs}\n\\label{sec:data}\n\\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Data, code and proofs}\n\nOur scripts, logs, Lean files and the text of this paper are openly\navailable at \\url{https://github.com/Alex-Linhares/MOSP_trough_SAT},\nunder \\repofile{paper3/}, with the companion Lean under\n\\repofile{lean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/}; the code and the\nproofs are open source under the MIT licence. Everything this paper\nreports can be re-run from there, and most of it can be checked without\nre-running. The repository holds:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item a script for every number in the paper, under\n  \\repofile{paper3/scripts/}, one per section\n  (Sections~\\ref{sec:statement}--\\ref{sec:related}); each reads the\n  manuscript's extract and the clone with plain file reads, executes\n  nothing of theirs, and has a \\texttt{--check} mode that verifies every\n  page, line and quoted fragment the section cites and exits non-zero\n  if any has moved; \\repofile{paper3/README.md} lists the command beside\n  each number;\n\\item the replay of Section~\\ref{sec:replay}: the two sandbox scripts\n  and the two second-checker scripts verbatim, the launch commands and\n  the timeline (\\repofile{paper3/replay/README.md}), the short logs,\n  the axiom output, the version lines and the hashes\n  (\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/}); the full build log and the exported\n  environment are too large to commit and are identified by their\n  SHA-256 hashes, which are committed, and a script\n  (\\repofile{paper3/replay/counts.sh}) that recomputes every count of\n  that section from the log;\n\\item the companion Lean of Section~\\ref{sec:lean}: the two files, the\n  axiom audit that prints the axioms of every declaration the paper\n  names (\\repofile{paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean}) with its committed\n  output, and the byte-level re-diff of the copied model against the\n  clone (\\repofile{paper3/lean/rediff.log}); Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}\n  lists the names;\n\\item the measurement of Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}: every call's\n  seconds and result (\\repofile{paper3/latex/tables/practitioner_raw.csv}),\n  the versions, machine and load of the run of record\n  (\\repofile{paper3/latex/tables/practitioner_meta.json}), and the\n  script that regenerates the formulas from their seeds and re-runs it;\n\\item the two audits the paper rests on, with their scripts and the\n  committed text extract of the manuscript\n  (\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/}), and the four public texts\n  Section~\\ref{sec:related} quotes, as fetched, with their hashes\n  (\\repofile{paper3/related/fetched/});\n\\item the record of every question the writing could not settle, one\n  numbered entry each (\\repofile{paper3/ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD}), and the\n  report of the blind reading of the whole paper against its sources\n  (\\repofile{paper3/refuter/}).\n\\end{itemize}\nThe object of the paper is not ours. The OpenAI collection is read-only\nat commit \\texttt{4eaee875} of \\url{https://github.com/openai/math}\n(Apache-2.0 licence); the challenge file, the solution tree, the scope\nnote and the manuscript are cited from it by file and line, and nothing\nin it was modified. A reader who wants to repeat the replay needs the\ncollection at that commit, the pinned Lean release and the Mathlib\nrevision Section~\\ref{sec:replay} names, a sandbox such as the one the\nscripts use, and about an hour of eight cores.\n\n\\paragraph{What this does not claim.} The statement above says what is\navailable and how it is checked, not that every reader will see the\nsame seconds: the timings of Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner} and the\nreplay's 33 minutes are one machine's, and only the counts, the hashes\nand the axiom lines are expected to repeat exactly.\nEOF\ncat > paper3/latex/sec12_ack.tex <<'EOF'\n% Acknowledgements, with the disclosure of AI use in paper 1's form\n% (paper1/latex/sec8_ack.tex), written by item 11 on 2026-11-05. The\n% software versions are those the sections record (Section 5 the replay,\n% Section 7 the measurement, Section 8 our library).\n\\section*{Acknowledgements}\n\\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Acknowledgements}\n\n\\paragraph{Use of AI tools.} Much of this work was carried out with Claude\nCode (Anthropic), an AI coding assistant running Claude models. Under the\nauthor's direction it wrote the scripts, the companion Lean files and the\ndrafts of the text, ran the sandboxed replay and the measurement, read\nthe manuscript and the Lean sources, and searched the few texts\nSection~\\ref{sec:related} names. The author chose the question, fixed\nthe title and the stance, directed the work, read the results and takes\nfull responsibility for the content of this paper. We did not take the\nAI's word for any result. The verified statement is what the Lean kernel\nand, in our replay, a second checker written independently of Lean\naccept (Section~\\ref{sec:replay}); every number in the paper is printed\nby a script that a reader can run (the data statement above); every\nquotation carries the page and line, or the file and line, where it can\nbe read; and the whole paper was read once more, against the clone, the\nextract, the audits and the logs, by a second session of the same\nassistant that had seen none of the drafts' sources, whose findings and\nwhat was done about each are on record\n(\\repofile{paper3/refuter/README.md}). ``People who checked'', in\nSection~\\ref{sec:intro}, means the author and that assistant.\n\n\\paragraph{The object of the paper.} The theorem, the proof and the\nmanuscript this paper reads are OpenAI's\n\\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026},\npublished under the Apache-2.0 licence in a collection whose checking\ntool \\citep{LeanComparator} and scope notes made the exact reading\npossible. Nothing in this paper is a claim of a flaw in that work.\n\n\\paragraph{Software.} The replay used the Lean release the collection\npins, Lean~4.34.1 \\citep{MouraUllrich2021}, with the Mathlib library\n\\citep{Mathlib2020} at the revision the collection pins, inside the\n\\texttt{bwrap} sandbox Section~\\ref{sec:replay} describes; the second\nchecker was \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib} \\citep{Nanoda} on an export written by\n\\texttt{lean4export} \\citep{Lean4Export}. Our own library builds with\nLean~4.34.0-rc2 and Mathlib (Section~\\ref{sec:lean}). The measurement\nused CaDiCaL~1.9.5 \\citep{Biere2024CaDiCaL} through PySAT\n\\citep{Ignatiev2018PySAT}, Ganak~2.8.0 \\citep{Sharma2019Ganak} and\nApproxMC~4.4.0 \\citep{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC, Soos2019BIRD}. The\nbibliography's DOIs were checked against Crossref and DataCite\n(\\repofile{paper3/latex/doi_check.log}).\nEOF\ncat >> paper3/latex/refs.bib <<'EOF'\n\n% Lean 4 and Mathlib, copied from paper1/latex/refs.bib (item 11, 2026-11-05)\n% for the acknowledgements' software paragraph; both DOIs checked there and\n% re-checked here by paper3/scripts/check_refs.py.\n@inproceedings{MouraUllrich2021,\n  author    = {de Moura, Leonardo and Ullrich, Sebastian},\n  title     = {The {Lean} 4 Theorem Prover and Programming Language},\n  booktitle = {Automated Deduction -- CADE 28},\n  series    = {Lecture Notes in Computer Science},\n  volume    = {12699},\n  pages     = {625--635},\n  year      = {2021},\n  doi       = {10.1007/978-3-030-79876-5_37}\n}\n\n@inproceedings{Mathlib2020,\n  author    = {{The mathlib Community}},\n  title     = {The {Lean} M