hlib\npackages. The 23 compatibility patches under lean/patches/ are for third-party packages\n(AINTLIB, PrimeNumberTheoremAnd, carleson, iut, . . . ) that this closure does not import.\nThe registry lean/formalization.yaml (lines 1683–1685) points at the same declaration and\nfile as the comparator’s JSON.\n\nB.5\n\nThe hypotheses in quantifier form\n\nThis is the detail behind Section 6.2. The property used throughout: for each fixed k ≥ 2 there is\none deterministic machine which, on the canonical encoding of any WidthAtMost k formula F\non n variables (comparator line 38), halts with countWord (count F) on its count tape (lines\n203–204, 147), the bit [0 < count F ] on its answer tape and in its halting state (lines 205–207),\nwithin C ( L + 1)d 2 Aρ(n) steps (lines 210–211). Since ρ(n)/n → 0 (Appendix A, A.6), for every\nδ > 0 there is an n0 (k, δ) beyond which the bound is below 2δn ; below n0 the bound is a constant\ndepending on k and δ. So the running time is Ok,δ (2δn ) for every δ > 0, and 2δn (n + m)Ok (1) with\nthe polynomial written out.\n62\n\n\fETH. In quantifiers: there is a δ > 0 such that no deterministic algorithm decides 3-SAT within\n2δn poly(n + m) steps on all inputs. Its negation: for every δ > 0 there is one. The verified\nstatement at k = 3 supplies, for every δ > 0, the same machine, whose answer bit is the decision,\nwithin Oδ (2δn ) poly(n + m) steps. This holds on every input of every size, so it refutes the\nhypothesis under either reading of “2Ω(n) ” (a lower bound at infinitely many n, or at all large n).\nIn the vocabulary of Impagliazzo and Paturi (2001), where sk is the infimum of the δ for which\nk-SAT has an O(2δn ) algorithm and ETH is s3 > 0: the verified statement gives sk = 0 for every\nk ≥ 2, by one algorithm per k.\nSETH. The manuscript’s form: for every ε ∈ (0, 1), there is a fixed k for which no deterministic\nalgorithm solves k-SAT in 2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1) time. The negation: there is an ε ∈ (0, 1) such that\nfor every fixed k there is a deterministic 2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1) algorithm. The verified statement\ngives more: for every ε ∈ (0, 1) and every k ≥ 2, the machine for k runs within Ck ( L + 1)dk 2 Ak ρ(n)\nsteps, and Ak ρ(n) ≤ (1 − ε)n for all n ≥ n0 (k, ε) because ρ(n) = o (n); below n0 the exponential\nfactor is a constant. So each k has a 2(1−ε)n (n + m)Ok (1) algorithm, and the polynomial’s degree\ndepending on k is exactly what the hypothesis’s “for a fixed k” permits. For the record, SETH\nimplies ETH: if 3-SAT had a 2o(n) algorithm then, by the Sparsification Lemma of Impagliazzo\net al. (2001), which reduces k-SAT on n variables to 2εn instances with Ok,ε (n) clauses and so, after\nthe clause-splitting reduction, to 3-SAT on Ok,ε (n) variables, so would k-SAT for every fixed k.\nGap-ETH. The negation of the manuscript’s form: for every δ, γ > 0 there is an O(2γn ) algorithm that separates a satisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment violates at least a δ\nfraction of the clauses, already at m = O(n). Exact decision separates them, since a formula in\nwhich every assignment violates a positive fraction of the clauses is unsatisfiable; and the verified machine at k = 3 decides within nO(1) 2 Aρ(n) = Oγ (2γn ) steps for every γ > 0, at every m (at\nm = O(n) the polynomial is O(n2d )). The promise is never used: the machine answers correctly\non every input, promised or not, with probability 1, which refutes the randomised form as well.\nNSETH. The negation of the manuscript’s form: there is an ε > 0 such that every fixed k\nhas a nondeterministic 2(1−ε)n (n + m)O(1) algorithm for unsatisfiability. The verified machine’s\nanswer bit is false exactly when count F = 0 (lines 205–207), so it decides unsatisfiability\ndeterministically within the SETH bound above for every ε and every k; and a deterministic\nmachine is a nondeterministic one whose only computation path is the deterministic run, so\nevery acceptance convention for nondeterministic time is met.\n#ETH. The