{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0146o6bweu5tScZcF91J1H5c","type":"tool_result","content":"Data, code and proofs\nOur scripts, logs, Lean files and the text of this paper are openly available at https://github.\ncom/Alex-Linhares/MOSP_trough_SAT, under paper3/, with the companion Lean under\nlean/MOSPFormalization/SubexpPathwidth/; the code and the proofs are open source\nunder the MIT licence. Everything this paper reports can be re-run from there, and most of it\ncan be checked without re-running. The repository holds:\n• a script for every number in the paper, under paper3/scripts/, one per section (Sections 2–9); each reads the manuscript’s extract and the clone with plain file reads, executes\nnothing of theirs, and has a -check mode that verifies every page, line and quoted fragment the section cites and exits non-zero if any has moved; paper3/README.md lists the\ncommand beside each number;\n• the replay of Section 5: the two sandbox scripts and the two second-checker scripts verbatim, the launch commands and the timeline (paper3/replay/README.md), the short\nlogs, the axiom output, the version lines and the hashes (paper3/replay/logs/); the\nfull build log and the exported environment are too large to commit and are identified by\ntheir SHA-256 hashes, which are committed, and a script (paper3/replay/counts.sh)\nthat recomputes every count of that section from the log;\n• the companion Lean of Section 8: the two files, the axiom audit that prints the axioms of\nevery declaration the paper names (paper3/lean/axiom_check.lean) with its committed output, and the byte-level re-diff of the copied model against the clone (paper3/lean/\nrediff.log); Appendix A lists the names;\n• the measurement of Section 7: every call’s seconds and result (paper3/latex/tables/\npractitioner_raw.csv), the versions, machine and load of the run of record (paper3/\nlatex/tables/practitioner_meta.json), and the script that regenerates the formulas from their seeds and re-runs it;\n• the two audits the paper rests on, with their scripts and the committed text extract of the\nmanuscript (reports/scratch/eth_audit/), and the four public texts Section 9 quotes,\nas fetched, with their hashes (paper3/related/fetched/);\n• the record of every question the writing could not settle, one numbered entry each, in\npaper3/ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD; and the report of the blind reading of the whole paper\nagainst its sources, in paper3/refuter/.\nThe object of the paper is not ours. The OpenAI collection is read-only at commit 4eaee875\nof https://github.com/openai/math (Apache-2.0 licence); the challenge file, the solution\ntree, the scope note and the manuscript are cited from it by file and line, and nothing in it was\nmodified. A reader who wants to repeat the replay needs the collection at that commit, the\npinned Lean release and the Mathlib revision Section 5 names, a sandbox such as the one the\nscripts use, and about an hour of eight cores.\n51\n\n\fWhat this does not claim. The statement above says what is available and how it is checked,\nnot that every reader will see the same seconds: the timings of Section 7 and the replay’s 33\nminutes are one machine’s, and only the counts, the hashes and the axiom lines are expected to\nrepeat exactly.\n\nAcknowledgements\nUse of AI tools. Much of this work was carried out with Claude Code (Anthropic), an AI\ncoding assistant running Claude models. Under the author’s direction it wrote the scripts, the\ncompanion Lean files and the drafts of the text, ran the sandboxed replay and the measurement,\nread the manuscript and the Lean sources, and searched the few texts Section 9 names. The\nauthor chose the question, fixed the title and the stance, directed the work, read the results\nand takes full responsibility for the content of this paper. We did not take the AI’s word for\nany result. The verified statement is what the Lean kernel and, in our replay, a second checker\nwritten independently of Lean accept (Section 5); every number in the paper is printed by a script\nthat a reader can run (the data statement above); every quotation carries the page and line, or\nthe file and line, where it can be read; and the whole paper was read once more, against the\nclone, the extract, the audits and the logs, by a second session of the same assistant that had\nseen none of the drafts’ sources, whose findings and what was done about each are on record\n(paper3/refuter/README.md). “People who checked”, in Section 1, means the author and\nthat assistant.\nThe object of the paper. The theorem, the proof and the manuscript this paper reads are OpenAI’s (OpenAI, 2026), published under the Apache-2.0 licence in a collection whose checking\ntool (Lean FRO, 2026b) and scope notes made the exact reading possible. Nothing in this paper\nis a claim of a flaw in that work.\nSoftware. The replay used the Lean release the collection pins, Lean 4.34.1 (de Moura and\nUllrich, 2021), with the Mathlib library (The mathlib Community, 2020) at the revision the collection pins, inside the bwrap sandbox Section 5 describes; the second checker was nanoda_lib\n(ammkrn, 2026) on an export written by lean4export (Lean FRO, 2026a). Our own library\nbuilds with Lean 4.34.0-rc2 and Mathlib (Section 8). The measurement used CaDiCaL 1.9.5 (Biere\net al., 2024) through PySAT (Ignatiev et al., 2018), Ganak 2.8.0 (Sharma et al., 2019) and ApproxMC 4.4.0 (Chakraborty et al., 2013; Soos and Meel, 2019). The bibliography’s DOIs were\nchecked against Crossref and DataCite (paper3/latex/doi_check.log).","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6bd812a1-f67e-4602-8dec-e9f4b911534a","uuid":"ac157c5d-f359-4295-aaad-097aaf5db990","timestamp":"2026-11-05T08:47:12.846Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Data, code and proofs\nOur scripts, logs, Lean files and the text of this paper are openly available at https://github.\ncom/Alex-Linhares/MOSP_trough_SAT, under paper3/, with the 